WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.220
Two out of the four Defenders have had their
solo series axed.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.640
Fans didn't have any warning before Luke Cage
and Iron Fist were cancelled, so why did Netflix

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.070
pull the plug on a pair of their Marvel shows?

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.780
Let's go over all the possible reasons.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.240
While it seems unlikely that sudden creative
differences could ever be the sole reason

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:22.860
why Luke Cage and Iron Fist failed to nab
third seasons within a week of each other,

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:27.130
The Hollywood Reporter claims that was part
of the issue, at least with regards to Luke

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:27.680
Cage.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.220
Considering both cancelled shows had managed
two solo seasons, it seems odd that the showrunners,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.830
Netflix, and Marvel suddenly hit back-to-back
speed bumps in their relationships that couldn't

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:38.320
be mended.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.400
Still, boiling creative tensions could definitely
be the straw that broke the camel's back if

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.400
there were other issues already in play.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:46.560
Speaking of play….

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.371
It's not a stretch to imagine that the impending
arrival of Disney's streaming service, Disney

00:00:51.371 --> 00:00:56.530
Play, is likely throwing a huge monkey wrench
into the works for all of Disney and Netflix's

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.010
joint TV properties.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.100
Given that Disney Play is arriving in 2019
and is set to be the exclusive host for almost

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.000
all of Disney's original content, including
Star Wars and Marvel projects, it makes sense

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.960
that this might be the first move by Disney
to bring its boys home.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:15.640
Since the company has already reclaimed Danny
and Luke, perhaps Disney is also gearing up

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.450
to take back Daredevil, Jessica Jones and
Punisher, once those properties' outstanding

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.640
Netflix obligations have run their course.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.800
If Disney really is terminating shows with
Netflix in order to have all its Marvel eggs

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.740
in one basket, the question remains: what
will Disney actually do with them?

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.720
The whole point of the Netflix shows was to
be an outlet for grittier stories within the

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:35.760
Marvel Universe.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.500
If Disney doesn't want to bring that kind
of edge over to their family-friendly streaming

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.900
service, could this be the end of Luke Cage
and Iron Fist for good?

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.560
At least on the Iron Fist side of things,
quality control has been a real issue.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.740
Both seasons 1 and 2 failed to win the world
over.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.060
Critics and fans alike just couldn't get behind
the show's slow pacing, mishandled protagonist,

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.940
and the scores of other issues.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.820
"You're scared...no."

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.260
Luke Cage definitely fared better with critics,
but it couldn't escape some of the wider cross-brand

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:13.290
complaints either.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.239
Bloated episodes and unnecessary plot threads
weighed down the storyline, proving that perhaps

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.520
Netflix's and Marvel's formulaic approach
to TV still needs some work.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:26.459
There's a chance the two companies recognized
this and determined that these shows were

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:30.689
sinking ships and that it was better to pull
the plug now rather than throw more good money

00:02:30.689 --> 00:02:31.180
at them.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:36.459
After Matt, Luke, Jessica, and Danny received
their big team-up season with The Defenders,

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:40.859
fans of the Netflix-Marvel-verse got excited
about future cross-brand opportunities.

00:02:40.859 --> 00:02:44.090
Would we get another team-up round with a
Defenders: Season 2?

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:47.999
Would the street-level heroes finally make
some sort of appearance in the Marvel Cinematic

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.500
Universe?

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.760
These questions swirled through fans' minds,
until… nothing happened.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.340
No second season of The Defenders ever materialized,
and the MCU still hasn't so much as admitted

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.980
the existence of the Netflix shows' characters.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.900
"Who the hell are you guys?"

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.200
This paints a pretty clear picture of where
the Netflix shows have been heading: nowhere.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.000
"You're goin' nowhere."

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:14.659
There's still no Defenders Season 2 in sight,
and it's not like Matt Murdock and his crew

00:03:14.659 --> 00:03:17.489
are going to get a big-screen spotlight in
a Marvel movie.

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:21.659
When you combine that with everything else,
it's no surprise Netflix and Marvel saw little

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:25.160
consequence in ending half of the b-list Avengers'
solo runs.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.620
Netflix doesn't release viewership numbers
publicly, so there's no way of knowing whether

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:33.709
those figures had a major impact on the decision
to end Iron Fist and Luke Cage.

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:39.420
Still, between over-saturation and brand stagnation,
there might very well have been a dip in viewership.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:44.840
Across all of Netflix's Marvel shows, 11 seasons'
worth of television were produced and released

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.420
between 2015 and 2018, with four of those
seasons debuting in 2018 alone.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:55.460
That's a lot of superhero shenanigans to stuff
down Marvel fans' throats, especially when

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:59.960
you consider that most of those seasons contained
copious amounts of padding and filler.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.000
Maybe people just got tired of watching Danny
punch things.

